      "id": "20261124T124431Z-677df4af#1",
      "line": 18,
      "path": "packages/belay/Belay/Sqlite/Programs/Scripts/RunResultSteps.lean",
      "rule": "R6",
      "severity": "should",
      "text": "`runScriptStep_output_of_resolved` is only about the model (`runScriptStep`, `resolveStatement` and `resolvedStepOutput` are all model declarations), but it is in the surface package. Fix: move it next to `runScriptStep_output` in `packages/belay-sqlite-model/Belay/Sqlite/Model/Programs/Scripts/QueryStepOutput.lean`."
    },
    {
      "id": "20261124T124431Z-677df4af#2",
      "line": 11,
      "path": "packages/belay/Belay/Sqlite/Programs/StatementRunLaws/SelectExecution.lean",
      "rule": "R2",
      "severity": "should",
      "text": "The restatements of `Run.send_unready` (line 11) and `Run.send_mustFail` (line 40) still say \"the step runs it at a clock value\", but the formula now assumes only an output equality and that `statementClock` of the named query statement gives a value. Fix: say that the clock of that query statement at index 0 has a value, and that the statement need not resolve to the query."
    },
    {
      "id": "20261124T124431Z-677df4af#3",
      "line": 19,
      "path": "packages/belay/Belay/Sqlite/Tooling/Counterexamples/SelectExecution.lean",
      "rule": "R2",
      "severity": "should",
      "text": "The restatement of `Run.send_returnsResult_select` says \"the step runs it at a clock value\" after the premise became an output equality, so it claims a resolution that the formula does not assume. Fix: restate the premise as \"`statementClock` of that query statement gives a value\" and drop the claim that the step runs the query."
    }
